Z={
1:"The question of saguṇa and nirguṇa shaped Indian religious life for centuries. In medieval North India, poet-saints split along this line: Kabīr and Guru Nānak sang of the formless, Tulsīdās and Sūrdās of Rāma and Kṛṣṇa with form. Both streams are honoured today. Tulsīdās himself wrote that there is no difference between the two, only in how the heart approaches.",
2:"Commentators note the three marks of the best devotee: the mind fixed on the Lord, constant union, and supreme faith. One traditional image compares such devotees to rivers that have already reached the sea and keep flowing into it. They do not wait for some future state; their union is daily and ongoing.",
3:"Madhusūdana Sarasvatī, an Advaitin who was also a passionate devotee of Kṛṣṇa, honoured both paths. He famously wrote that he knew of no reality higher than Kṛṣṇa with the flute, his face like the full moon, even though his philosophy was non-dual. His life is often cited to show that the formless and the personal need not compete.",
4:"Commentators note that sanniyamya, “restraining”, comes first: the formless path demands unusual self-control. Śaṅkara’s own life is cited as an example. He renounced home as a boy, travelled the length of India, and taught the formless Brahman. Yet he also wrote hymns to Śiva, Viṣṇu and the Goddess for ordinary seekers.",
5:"Dehavat, “embodied”, is the key. Commentators note that as long as we feel ourselves to be a body, we naturally relate to God through form, name and story. Vivekananda compared it to a child learning to write with large letters before small ones. The form is not a lesser truth but the right door for most people now.",
6:"Commentators note the word ananyena, “with nothing else”: the yoga is undivided. Śrī Vaiṣṇava commentators explain that this means not seeking other goals while worshipping the Lord. Many devotees find this hard in practice, because prayers often carry requests. The Gītā does not reject such prayers (7.16), but praises undivided love as best.",
7:"Saṃsāra is often pictured as an ocean: deep, endless, with waves of birth and death, currents of desire and sharks of anger. The Lord is called bhava-sāgara-tāraka, the one who carries across the ocean of becoming. Ādi Śaṅkara’s hymn Bhaja Govindam ends with a plea to cross this ocean by the Lord’s grace.",
8:"Commentators note the promise nivasiṣyasi mayy eva, “you will live in me”. Rāmānuja reads it as living in the Lord’s presence after this life. Śaṅkara reads it as abiding in the Self. Teachers say that when mind and understanding both rest in the Lord, the devotee no longer needs to go anywhere; he is already home.",
9:"Indian tradition has many forms of practice for those whose minds wander. Japa with a rosary, mālā, of 108 beads gives the hand something to do while the mind returns to the name. Kīrtan, group singing, carries the mind along. Tulsī and Vaiṣṇava saints recommended the name as the easiest practice of all in the present age.",
10:"The life of Hanumān is often given as an example of devotion through work. He did not meditate in a cave; he crossed the ocean, found Sītā, carried a mountain of herbs and served Rāma in every task. His whole life was mat-karma, work for the Lord. Commentators say such service is a complete path, not a lesser one.",
11:"Commentators note yatātmavān, “self-controlled”: even letting go of fruits requires discipline. Madhusūdana says this rung is for those whose minds cannot yet turn to the Lord directly. Their honest work, offered without grasping, slowly prepares them. Many working people find this verse reassuring: the path is open even in a busy life.",
12:"Some commentators, like Madhusūdana, read the verse as praising letting go because it ends the restlessness that disturbs all other practices. Peace makes knowledge and meditation easier. Gandhi took this verse as a summary of his own practice: do the work, renounce the fruit, and find peace in the doing.",
13:"Commentators note that adveṣṭā, not hating, is the first quality of the beloved devotee. The Bhāgavata tells of Prahlāda, who did not hate his father even when his father tried to kill him. When Narasiṃha offered him any boon, he asked that his father be forgiven. He is the classic example of this verse.",
14:"Commentators note dṛḍha-niścaya, “of firm resolve”: contentment is not passivity. The devotee is steady and decisive. Dhruva, the boy who sat in meditation for months to see Viṣṇu, is often cited for firm resolve. When the Lord appeared, he found that he no longer wanted the kingdom he had come for; contentment had replaced it.",
15:"Commentators find this verse one of the most practical in the Gītā for family and community life. The Tamil classic Tirukkuṟaḷ has a similar teaching: the best revenge on those who harm you is to shame them by doing them good. A person who neither disturbs nor is disturbed brings peace wherever they go.",
16:"Commentators note udāsīna, “unconcerned”, used of the Lord himself in 9.9. The devotee shares the Lord’s serene detachment. Rāmānuja says such a devotee is not anxious about outcomes, because he trusts the Lord. Gata-vyatha, “free from distress”: he has handed over his worries. Competence without anxiety is the ideal.",
17:"Commentators note that bhaktimān, “full of devotion”, closes the verse: the freedom from reactions is not cold. It comes from love. The saint Tukārām of Maharashtra lost his wealth, family and reputation in a famine and in disputes, yet remained full of joy, singing to Viṭṭhala. His life is often cited for this verse.",
18:"Commentators note that cold and heat, physical opposites, are listed beside honour and dishonour, social ones. Both are passing weather. Śaṅkara says the devotee bears them without inner disturbance. Many saints faced insults calmly. Saint Eknāth of Paithan, according to legend, was spat on many times by a man as he returned from bathing, and each time simply bathed again without anger, until the man begged forgiveness.",
19:"Commentators note sthira-matiḥ, “steady in mind”, at the end of the description. Śaṅkara says the steadiness comes from knowing the truth. Rāmānuja says it comes from fixing the mind on the Lord. The description of 12.13–19 is often recited as a daily self-examination: which of these qualities did I live today?",
20:"Commentators note that the chapter ends with śraddadhānāḥ, “those with faith”, and mat-paramāḥ, “holding me as supreme”. These are the same marks as 12.2, closing the circle. Many traditions recite chapter 12 daily. In Gandhi’s ashram it was among the chapters chanted at evening prayer. It is short enough to learn by heart.",
}
